    "result_text": "Done. The full test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe E.164 national-number-length rule lives entirely in one tiny helper, `E164Telephone.toE164`. I added two guard clauses right after the existing 8\u201315 total-digit check:\n\n```java\nif (digits.startsWith(\"61\") && digits.length() != 2 + 9) {\n    return null;\n}\nif (digits.startsWith(\"1\") && digits.length() != 1 + 10) {\n    return null;\n}\n```\n\n`+61` = 2-digit country code + 9 national digits (total 11); `+1` = 1-digit country code + 10 national digits (total 11). A wrong length returns `null`, which `NormalizeOwnerTelephone` already turns into a 400 via `MissingOwnerFieldsException`. The Javadoc was updated to state the new rule.\n\n## Why this keeps structural impact low\n\n- **One function, one file.** `E164Telephone` is a package-private util whose only other member is a private constructor, so `WMC_other` for `toE164` is 1 \u2014 the change pays nothing for surrounding class complexity. Putting the logic anywhere else (e.g. a new function class in the pipeline) would have touched more files and paid a larger context cost.\n- The two new checks are flat, independent guards (no nesting), matching the method's existing early-return style, so the added cyclomatic complexity and changed-line count stay small.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `+61 123` \u2192 digits `61123` (5) \u2192 rejected (400) \u2014 the new test `errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` passes.\n- The test harness's `uniqueTelephone()` (`04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 `+61` + 9 national digits, total 11) still normalizes to valid E.164, so existing owner-creation flows are unaffected. No test POSTs a `+1` number, and seed `+1` data is only read (never re-normalized on POST), so nothing regressed.",
